G='Legendary heroes & mythological figures'
H=[
("Perseus",-1300,"Legendary; myths recorded from c. 700 BCE","Mythological Greek hero","Argos and Mycenae, Greece","Slaying Medusa; rescuing Andromeda","In Greek myth, Perseus was the son of Zeus and the princess Danaë, who was locked away by her father Acrisius because of a prophecy that her son would kill him; mother and baby were set adrift in a chest. Raised on the island of Seriphos, he was sent by King Polydectes to bring back Medusa's head. Using Athena's mirrored shield, Hermes' winged sandals, a cap of invisibility and a magic bag, he beheaded Medusa, then rescued the Ethiopian princess Andromeda from the sea monster Cetus and married her. He later accidentally killed Acrisius with a discus, fulfilling the prophecy, and was said to have founded Mycenae. He and Andromeda became constellations. He is a mythological figure, not a historical one.","Perseus","Medusa and the Gorgons; Pegasus; Greek religion and mythology; Mycenaean Greece"),
("Heracles",-1300,"Legendary; worshipped across the Greek and Roman world (Roman Hercules)","Mythological Greek hero","Thebes and Tiryns, Greece","The Twelve Labours","Son of Zeus and the mortal Alcmene, Heracles was hated by Hera, who drove him mad so that he killed his own family. To atone he served King Eurystheus and performed twelve labours: killing the Nemean lion and the Hydra, capturing the Cretan bull and the guard-dog Cerberus, cleaning the Augean stables by diverting rivers, and more. He also killed centaurs and freed Prometheus. Poisoned by the blood of the centaur Nessus, he burned himself on a pyre and became a god. Alexander the Great claimed descent from him, and Hercules became a model of strength in art.","Heracles","Hydra; Cerberus; Centaur; Greek religion and mythology; Alexander the Great"),
("Theseus",-1300,"Legendary; mythical king of Athens","Mythological Greek hero","Athens","Killing the Minotaur","Theseus, son of King Aegeus of Athens (or of Poseidon), cleared the road to Athens of bandits, then went to Crete as part of the tribute to King Minos. With Ariadne's thread he killed the Minotaur in the Labyrinth and escaped, but abandoned Ariadne on Naxos and forgot to change his black sails, so his father leapt into the sea. Athenians credited him with uniting Attica into one city-state.","Theseus","Minotaur; Minoan civilisation; Greek religion and mythology"),
("Bellerophon",-1300,"Legendary; myth in Homer's Iliad","Mythological Greek hero","Corinth and Lycia","Killing the Chimera on Pegasus","Bellerophon tamed the winged horse Pegasus with a golden bridle from Athena and killed the fire-breathing Chimera for King Iobates of Lycia. Proud of his success, he tried to ride Pegasus to Olympus; Zeus sent a gadfly, Pegasus threw him, and he spent his life wandering lame and alone.","Bellerophon","Chimera; Pegasus; Homer; Greek religion and mythology"),
("Odysseus",-1200,"Legendary; hero of Homer's Odyssey (8th century BCE)","Mythological Greek hero, king of Ithaca","Ithaca, Greece","The Trojan Horse; ten-year voyage home","Odysseus devised the wooden horse that ended the Trojan War. On his ten-year journey home he blinded the Cyclops Polyphemus, resisted the Sirens' song by being tied to the mast, escaped the witch Circe and the monsters Scylla and Charybdis, and finally returned to Ithaca to defeat the suitors of his wife Penelope.","Odysseus","Cyclops; Trojan War (traditional); Homer; Greek religion and mythology"),
("Jason",-1300,"Legendary; myth told in Apollonius of Rhodes' Argonautica (3rd century BCE)","Mythological Greek hero","Iolcus, Greece","The Golden Fleece","Jason led the Argonauts on the ship Argo to Colchis (Georgia) to fetch the Golden Fleece, guarded by a dragon. With the help of the sorceress Medea, he succeeded, but later abandoned her, leading to tragedy. Some historians link the story to Greek trade with the Black Sea and gold panning with fleeces in Georgia.","Jason","Dragon; Greek religion and mythology"),
("Beowulf",500,"Legendary; poem composed c. 700–1000 CE, set in 6th-century Scandinavia","Hero of the Old English epic","Geatland (southern Sweden) and Denmark","Killing Grendel, Grendel's mother and a dragon","Beowulf, a Geatish warrior, comes to help the Danish king Hrothgar, whose hall Heorot is attacked by the monster Grendel; he tears off Grendel's arm, then kills Grendel's mother in her underwater lair. Fifty years later, as king, he fights a treasure-guarding dragon and dies. The poem survives in a single manuscript in the British Library and was studied by J. R. R. Tolkien.","Beowulf_(hero)","Dragon; Beowulf manuscript; Old English; Vikings"),
("Saint George",280,"c. 280–303 CE (historical martyr); dragon legend from the 11th–13th centuries","Christian saint and soldier","Cappadocia and Palestine","Slaying the dragon","George was probably a Roman soldier executed under Diocletian around 303 for refusing to renounce Christianity. The dragon story, popularised by the Golden Legend (13th century), tells of him killing a dragon that demanded a princess as sacrifice in Libya. He is patron saint of England, Georgia, Ethiopia and many other places.","Saint_George","Dragon; Christianity; Roman Empire"),
("Sigurd",500,"Legendary; Völsunga saga and Poetic Edda (13th century)","Norse hero","Scandinavia and Germany","Killing the dragon Fáfnir","Sigurd (Siegfried in German) killed the dragon Fáfnir guarding a cursed treasure; tasting its blood, he understood birds. The Nibelungenlied and Wagner's Ring cycle retell his story.","Sigurd","Dragon; Norse religion; Vikings"),
("Gilgamesh",-2700,"c. 2700 BCE (possibly a historical king of Uruk); epic from c. 2100 BCE","King of Uruk and epic hero","Uruk, Mesopotamia","The Epic of Gilgamesh","Gilgamesh was probably a real king of Uruk, later mythologised as two-thirds god. With his friend Enkidu he killed the giant Humbaba and the Bull of Heaven. After Enkidu's death he searched for immortality, met the flood survivor Utnapishtim, and found and lost a plant of rejuvenation to a snake.","Gilgamesh","Epic of Gilgamesh; Sumer; Giants; Mesopotamian religion"),
("Minamoto no Raikō",948,"948–1021","Samurai warrior","Japan","Killing the oni Shuten-dōji","Minamoto no Yorimitsu (Raikō) was a real Heian-period warrior. Legends say he and his four retainers disguised themselves as monks, drugged the oni king Shuten-dōji with sake and beheaded him on Mount Ōe; he also killed a giant spider.","Minamoto_no_Yorimitsu","Oni; Heian Japan"),
("Vikramaditya",-57,"Legendary king of Ujjain; the Vikram Samvat era begins in 57 BCE","Legendary Indian king","Ujjain","Vikram and Betaal","Vikramaditya is a legendary king famed for wisdom and justice, possibly inspired by Gupta emperor Chandragupta II. In the Vetala tales he carries a riddling vetala from a cremation ground 25 times.","Vikramaditya","Vetala (Betaal); Chandragupta II; Hinduism"),
("King Solomon",-970,"c. 970–931 BCE (traditional)","King of Israel","Jerusalem","Wisdom; the First Temple","In the Hebrew Bible, Solomon, son of David, built the First Temple in Jerusalem and was famed for wisdom. In Islamic and Jewish tradition he commanded the jinn, who built for him, using a ring with a divine seal.","Solomon","Djinn (Jinn); Ancient Israel and Judah; Judaism; Islam"),
("Rabbi Judah Loew",1520,"c. 1520–1609","Rabbi and scholar","Prague","The Golem of Prague legend","Judah Loew ben Bezalel, the Maharal, was a real Talmudic scholar in Prague. A later legend says he created a clay golem to protect the Jews of Prague.","Judah_Loew_ben_Bezalel","Golem; Judaism; Holy Roman Empire"),
]
